Yeah. We all get a partial view, and we all fill in gaps. “Narrative hallucination” is a good metaphor for the moment the story we filled in starts to feel like something we directly witnessed.

Say two people leave the same dinner. One noticed a friend go quiet after a joke and thought, I offended him. The other saw him check his phone just before he went quiet and thought, He got bad news. Both saw something real. Each supplied a cause. And each might remember the evening later with that cause already baked in.

That’s where in twos gets interesting for Pantheon. Put the two accounts side by side and keep track of what each person actually saw. The comparison might surface a detail neither person knew mattered. The silence was visible to both. The phone was visible to one. What the friend was feeling was still an open question.